<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jaan Anvelt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1884">1884</a>–<a href="/years/1937">1937</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jaan Anvelt was an Estonian Bolshevik leader who chaired the Commune of the Working People of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, proclaimed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narva">Narva</a> in November 1918 with Soviet Russian backing during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonian-war-of-independence">Estonian War of Independence</a>. After the Commune collapsed in 1919 he directed the communist underground, including the failed Tallinn uprising of 1924, from Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. He was arrested during Stalin's purges and died in custody in 1937.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jaan-anvelt.jpg" alt="Jaan Anvelt" width="320" height="442" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jaan_Anvelt_1925.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
